    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. The Short Answer: Treadmill Brands Ranked by What You Need</a>
          <a href="#framework" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. How to Judge a Treadmill Brand</a>
          <a href="#warranty-and-service" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Warranty and Service: The Brand Signal That Matters Most</a>
          <a href="#subscriptions-and-tiers" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Subscriptions and Price Tiers Across Brands</a>
          <a href="#nordictrack" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. NordicTrack: The Interactive-Training Brand</a>
          <a href="#proform" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. ProForm: iFIT Features at a Lower Price</a>
          <a href="#sole" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Sole: Hardware First, No Required Subscription</a>
          <a href="#horizon" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Horizon: Mid-Range, Still Being Verified</a>
          <a href="#commercial-brands" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Life Fitness and Matrix: Commercial Pedigree at Home</a>
          <a href="#bowflex" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Bowflex: Treadmills and the Max Trainer</a>
          <a href="#walking-pad-brands" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Budget Walking-Pad Brands, as a Category</a>
          <a href="#choosing" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Using Brand to Narrow the Choice</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">13. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Brand matters less than most shoppers think and more than spec sheets admit. Less, because
          every major brand sells machines across a wide range of quality, and a brand's budget
          model can be worse than a rival's mid-range one. More, because what happens after the sale
          — warranty terms, who turns up to fix it, whether parts exist in eight years, and whether
          the console works without a subscription — is largely set at brand level.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer: Treadmill Brands Ranked by What You Need</h2>
<p>
            Ranked by the buyer each brand suits best, among the brands we cover in depth:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Interactive, trainer-led training:</strong> NordicTrack, built around the
            iFIT platform, with the widest range of touchscreen machines.</li>
            <li><strong>iFIT features at a lower price:</strong> ProForm, NordicTrack's sister brand,
            which generally trades some hardware for price.</li>
            <li><strong>Strong hardware with no required subscription:</strong> Sole, which our
            guides consistently rate well for motor and frame per dollar.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial-grade durability at home:</strong> Life Fitness and Matrix
            Fitness, gym-equipment manufacturers with home lines built on commercial engineering.</li>
            <li><strong>Mid-range value:</strong> Horizon, a Johnson Health Tech brand like Matrix,
            which we are still completing a full guide for.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking while you work:</strong> the budget walking-pad brands, as a category
            — convenient and inexpensive, with shorter expected lifespans and thinner support.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Bowflex is a well-known fitness brand that shoppers often ask about in this context; it
            now sells three folding treadmills alongside the better-known Max Trainer, and our
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex page</a> explains where it
            stands. Within any brand, choose the model by the specifications that matter for you —
            motor, deck, weight capacity — using our
            <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying guide</a>.
            Brand narrows the field; it does not choose the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="framework" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How to Judge a Treadmill Brand</h2>
<p>
            Five things separate brands in ways that individual spec sheets do not show. They are
            the questions we ask of every brand we cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty terms.</strong> Not the headline frame warranty, which most brands make
            long, but the motor, parts and labour terms — and whether labour means a technician in
            your home or a box of parts on your doorstep. These terms show how much of the cost of a
            failure the brand is willing to carry.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Service network.</strong> Whether the brand has technicians, its own or
            contracted, who will come to a house in your area, and how long owners typically wait.
            A strong warranty with nobody to honour it locally is weaker than it reads.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts availability.</strong> Whether belts, controller boards, motors and
            consoles remain available years after a model is discontinued. Brands with large installed
            bases and commercial businesses tend to support parts longer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Subscription dependence.</strong> What the console does with no membership
            active. Some brands build their range around a platform; others treat apps as optional
            extras. Neither is wrong, but the cost and the long-term usefulness of the machine differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price tiers and consistency.</strong> Where the brand's range sits, and whether
            quality scales predictably across it. A brand whose cheapest and dearest machines are
            clearly related is easier to buy from than one whose entry models share only the badge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-service" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Warranty and Service: The Brand Signal That Matters Most</h2>
<p>
            A warranty is a manufacturer's estimate, in money, of how its machine will hold up. A
            brand that gives long motor cover and multi-year in-home labour is betting it will rarely
            have to pay. A brand that gives a lifetime frame and ninety days of labour is telling you
            something different, in larger type.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare brands on the terms that decide what a repair costs you: the motor, the parts —
            which usually means the controller board, incline motor and electronics — and labour.
            Check whether in-home service is included or whether you fit replacement parts yourself.
            Check whether registration is required, and whether wear items are excluded. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains each tier.
          </p>
          <p>
            Service is the part warranties do not show. Owner experiences of support vary widely
            within every brand, and some of the most common complaints across the category concern
            wait times for technicians and parts rather than the machines themselves. Before buying,
            ask the retailer who services that brand in your area, and search for recent owner
            reports about support specifically. A brand with a slightly shorter warranty and a
            responsive local service network can be the better buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscriptions-and-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Subscriptions and Price Tiers Across Brands</h2>
<p>
            The clearest split between treadmill brands today is how they treat software. On one
            side are brands whose machines are designed around a subscription training platform:
            trainer-led classes, automatic incline and speed control during workouts, and large
            touchscreens. On the other are brands whose consoles do everything essential on their
            own, with apps as an optional extra.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither approach is better in the abstract. The platform approach suits people who find
            structure and variety motivating and will pay for it continuously. The hardware-first
            approach suits people who will watch their own screen, or who do not want a recurring
            cost attached to a machine they expect to own for a decade. The question for each brand
            is what its consoles do with no subscription active, model by model. Our guides to
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a>
            and <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            cover both sides.
          </p>
          <p>
            Price tiers matter because every brand stretches its name across a range. Budget
            machines from a premium brand share its badge but often not its build, and a strong
            mid-range brand can beat a famous one at the same price. Compare machines at the same
            price across brands, not across a brand's range. Our pages on
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill costs</a>
            and <a href="/cheap-vs-premium-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cheap versus premium</a>
            cover what changes as price rises.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nordictrack" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. NordicTrack: The Interactive-Training Brand</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack is owned by iFIT Health &amp; Fitness, formerly ICON Health &amp; Fitness,
            which also owns ProForm. Its treadmill range is built around the iFIT platform:
            touchscreen consoles, trainer-led workouts and automatic incline and speed control are the
            brand's defining features, and much of its lineup is designed to be used with a
            membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strengths:</strong> the broadest range of interactive machines from a single
            brand, from budget folding models to heavier non-folding machines and steep incline
            trainers, with build quality that scales fairly predictably with price.
            <strong>Things to weigh:</strong> the ongoing cost of the subscription, what each
            console does without it, and owner reports of mixed support experiences. Warranties are
            competitive at frame level, with shorter parts and labour terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            NordicTrack suits buyers who want structured, trainer-led sessions and have priced the
            membership over several years. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack brand guide</a>
            covers the lineup tier by tier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="proform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. ProForm: iFIT Features at a Lower Price</h2>
<p>
            ProForm is NordicTrack's sister brand under the same parent company, and runs the same
            iFIT software. It is positioned to reach more budget-conscious shoppers: on comparable
            models the spec sheets can look similar, while the differences tend to sit underneath, in
            frame and motor construction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strengths:</strong> access to the iFIT ecosystem at lower prices, and a range
            that suits walkers and moderate joggers well. <strong>Things to weigh:</strong> the
            hardware is generally lighter-duty than NordicTrack's heavier lines or the
            hardware-focused brands, and the same subscription questions apply — check what each
            model does without a membership, since some machines bundle one and lean on it more than
            others.
          </p>
          <p>
            ProForm suits buyers who want interactive training, will mostly walk or jog, and want to
            spend less than NordicTrack's heavier machines cost. Our
            <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm brand guide</a>
            covers the range, and
            <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack versus ProForm</a>
            compares the two directly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sole" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Sole: Hardware First, No Required Subscription</h2>
<p>
            Sole has built its reputation on durable, no-subscription-required treadmills with
            strong motors and cushioning for their price. Its consoles run speed, incline and
            built-in programmes without any membership; its app is optional workout tracking rather
            than a gate on features.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strengths:</strong> base hardware per dollar, warranty terms that are strong for
            the home market — particularly on the frame and motor — and a reputation for good long-term owner
            satisfaction. <strong>Things to weigh:</strong> the console experience is
            more basic than a touchscreen platform's, and buyers who want guided video classes will
            need to bring their own screen and app.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sole suits buyers who prioritise motor, deck and frame over screen, and who would rather
            not attach a subscription to their treadmill. Our
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole Fitness brand guide</a>
            covers the lineup and warranty in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="horizon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Horizon: Mid-Range, Still Being Verified</h2>
<p>
            Horizon Fitness is part of Johnson Health Tech, the same group that owns Matrix. It sells
            home treadmills aimed mainly at the mid-range, and is frequently compared with Sole and
            ProForm by shoppers at those prices.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are deliberately cautious here. Our full Horizon guide is still being completed while
            we verify current model designations, warranty terms and subscription positions, and we
            would rather not assert brand-wide claims we have not confirmed. If you are considering
            a Horizon machine, apply the framework above directly: check the continuous motor rating,
            deck size and weight capacity against your needs, read the motor, parts and labour terms,
            ask who services it locally, and confirm what the console does without any app.
          </p>
          <p>
            That approach works for any brand we have not yet covered in depth. A machine that
            passes those checks is a good buy whatever its badge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="commercial-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Life Fitness and Matrix: Commercial Pedigree at Home</h2>
<p>
            Life Fitness and Matrix Fitness are primarily commercial gym-equipment manufacturers —
            the brands behind many machines in health clubs, hotels and university gyms — and both
            sell home lines built on that engineering. Matrix is a brand of Johnson Health Tech; Life
            Fitness has made commercial equipment since the late 1960s.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strengths:</strong> commercial construction philosophy, typically including AC
            drive motors and heavy fixed frames, built for far heavier use than most homes will
            impose; warranty terms that are generally among the stronger in the home market; and the
            parts support that comes with a large commercial installed base. Neither requires a
            subscription for core functions. <strong>Things to weigh:</strong> they cost meaningfully
            more than mass-market brands, do not fold, are heavy to deliver, and may have electrical
            or installation requirements to check.
          </p>
          <p>
            These brands suit households with heavy, shared or high-mileage use, and buyers who want a
            gym-grade feel. Our <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a>
            and <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix Fitness</a>
            brand guides cover their home lineups, and
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmills for home</a>
            covers the practicalities of commercial machines in a house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bowflex" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Bowflex: Treadmills and the Max Trainer</h2>
<p>
            Bowflex is one of the best-known names in home fitness, built originally on strength
            equipment and now owned by Johnson Health Tech, which also owns Horizon and Matrix. It
            sells three folding treadmills — the T6, T9 and T16 — each marketed as working without a
            subscription; our <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6 review</a> covers the entry
            model. It is still best known in cardio for the Max Trainer, a low-impact
            elliptical-stepper hybrid with a JRNY coaching app that some shoppers consider as an
            alternative to a treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whether a Max Trainer is a sensible alternative depends on the goal. For lower-impact
            cardio it can be a strong option; for anyone who specifically wants to run or walk, it is
            a different kind of machine. Our
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex guide</a> covers the full
            range and compares its Max Trainer models, and
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill versus elliptical</a>
            covers the broader choice between those two formats.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pad-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Budget Walking-Pad Brands, as a Category</h2>
<p>
            The fastest-growing part of the market is compact under-desk walking pads, sold largely
            online under a wide and changing set of brand names. We treat them as a category rather
            than profiling each brand, because the trade-offs are shared and the names come and go.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strengths:</strong> low prices, small footprints, easy storage, and genuinely good
            at the job they are designed for — slow walking, often at a desk. <strong>Things to
            weigh:</strong> smaller motors working at a high duty cycle, thinner decks and lighter
            rollers mean expected lifespans measured in a few years of daily use rather than a decade;
            warranties are typically short; and service networks and long-term parts availability are
            often thin or absent. Some models appear under several brand names, which makes it harder
            to judge a brand's track record.
          </p>
          <p>
            Judge a walking-pad brand on the same framework, scaled down: a warranty that includes the
            motor, a responsive support channel, a published weight capacity with headroom, and a
            return window you can actually use. Our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>
            covers the category, the
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            covers what to check before buying one, and
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill</a>
            covers when one is the right choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a worked example of reading one of these listings critically — which figures are the seller's claims, which are disputed and what the warranty really covers — see our <a href="/wellfit-tm037-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Wellfit TM037 review</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Using Brand to Narrow the Choice</h2>
<p>
            Brand is most useful as a first filter. Decide what kind of owner you will be — someone
            who wants guided classes, someone who wants durable hardware and no recurring cost, or
            someone whose household will push a machine hard — and that points to two or three
            brands. Then compare machines from those brands at the same price, on the specifications
            that matter: continuous motor rating, deck size, weight capacity and the full warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. First, a brand's reputation was earned by some of its machines, not all; a
            famous name on an entry-level model buys you less than it appears to. Second, brands
            change — ownership, warranty terms and subscription policies all move — so a brand
            ranking is a starting point that needs checking against the specific machine and the
            current terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">brands hub</a> lists every brand
            guide we publish, our <a href="/best-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills</a>
            roundup compares machines across brands, and
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            explains why build and support matter more over a decade than any single feature.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the best treadmill brands?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what you need. Among the brands we cover, NordicTrack leads for interactive, trainer-led training; ProForm offers the same iFIT platform at lower prices; Sole is strong on hardware without a required subscription; and Life Fitness and Matrix bring commercial engineering to the home. Choose the brand by need, then the model by specifications.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What treadmill brands are ranked highest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single ranking that suits everyone, which is why we rank by need. For durability with no subscription, Sole, Life Fitness and Matrix rank highly in our guides; for interactive features, NordicTrack and ProForm. Judge any brand on warranty terms, local service, parts availability, subscription dependence and consistency across its price range.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which treadmill brand is most reliable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reliability varies more between models than between brands, since every brand sells machines across a wide quality range. Brands with commercial businesses, such as Life Fitness and Matrix, generally build their home lines to heavier-duty standards. Long motor and labour warranties and strong parts support are the best brand-level signs of expected reliability.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is NordicTrack or Sole better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They suit different buyers. NordicTrack is built around the iFIT platform and suits people who want trainer-led classes and will pay for the membership. Sole focuses on motor, frame and cushioning per dollar with no required subscription. Compare models at the same price on continuous motor rating, deck size, weight capacity and full warranty terms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are NordicTrack and ProForm the same company?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are sister brands owned by the same parent, iFIT Health & Fitness, formerly ICON Health & Fitness, and both run iFIT software. ProForm is generally positioned at lower prices, with differences tending to show up in frame and motor construction rather than on the spec sheet. Our NordicTrack versus ProForm comparison covers the details.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap walking pad brands any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For slow walking at a desk, many are fine and good value. Expect a shorter lifespan than a full treadmill, typically short warranties, and thinner service and parts support, with some models sold under several brand names. Choose one with a warranty covering the motor, a published weight capacity with headroom, and a usable return window.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Choosing a Treadmill Brand</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Brand loyalty is a reasonable shortcut until it replaces checking the machine itself.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the badge, not the model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Every major brand sells machines across a wide quality range. An entry-level model from a premium brand often shares its name but not its build, and can be outclassed by a mid-range machine from a less famous one. Compare machines at the same price across brands.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging warranties on the frame alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most brands offer a long frame warranty. The terms that separate them are motor, parts and labour, and whether service happens in your home. Compare those, and ask who services the brand in your area.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring what the console does without a subscription</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some brands design their machines around a paid platform. That suits many buyers, but check what each specific model does with no membership, and price the subscription over the years you expect to own the machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a ranking as permanent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Ownership, warranty terms, subscription policies and service arrangements change. A brand ranking is a starting point; confirm the current terms for the specific machine with the retailer before you buy.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

